.TH ROSTER 1 "" "roster" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
roster \- give this box (and Claude) access to the hosts in roster.yml
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
roster init [\-\-blank | \-\-inventory HOW] [\-\-name NAME]
roster setup [\-\-only LIST | \-\-host NAME] [\-\-dry\-run] [\-\-force] [\-\-key\-type TYPE] [\-\-rotate]
roster sync [\-\-no\-vault]
roster check [\-\-box] [\-\-auth] [\-\-only LIST | \-\-host NAME] [\-\-json] [host]
roster hosts [\-\-resources] [\-\-only LIST] [\-\-find TEXT] [\-\-all] [\-\-long] [\-\-json] [NAME ...]
roster run [\-\-sudo] host ...
roster add [\-\-ip ADDR] [\-\-user USER] [\-\-auth REF] [\-\-group GROUP] [\-\-enroll | \-\-no\-enroll] [\-\-os {windows,posix}] [\-\-title TEXT] [\-\-type TEXT] [\-\-port N] [\-\-from\-vault] [\-\-json] [\-\-all] [\-\-mark\-seen] [\-\-no\-sync] [\-\-setup] [\-\-dry\-run] [\-y] [NAME]
roster discover [\-\-write] [\-\-group NAME] [\-\-ports PORTS] [\-\-top\-ports N] [\-\-no\-ping] [\-\-os] [\-\-builtin] [\-\-timeout SECONDS] [\-\-force] [\-\-json] CIDR [CIDR ...]
roster web [\-d BODY] [\-H HEADER] [\-k] [\-\-timeout SECONDS] HOST [SERVICE] METHOD PATH [HOST [SERVICE] METHOD PATH ...]
roster mesh [\-\-dry\-run] [\-\-rotate HOST] [\-y] [\-\-all] [\-\-remove] [\-\-json] [{cut,status,leftovers}] [HOST]
roster revoke [\-\-only LIST] [\-\-all] [\-y]
roster inv [\-\-json] {list,add,use,remove,which} [NAME] [PATH]
roster vault ACTION ...
roster vault status [\-\-json]
roster vault sync
roster vault unlock [PROVIDER]
roster vault lock [PROVIDER]
roster vault reveal [\-\-wifi] name
roster vault bw [\-\-write] ...
roster vault op [\-\-write] ...
roster help [\-\-man] [\-\-install] [\-\-prefix DIR] [\-\-completion SHELL] [COMMAND ...]
roster serve
roster askpass [prompt]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Give this box (and Claude) access to the hosts in roster.yml.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBOne source of truth for the team.\fR The host list lives in git and the logins live in the shared vault. Adding a host takes one change to \fBroster.yml\fR, and rotating a password takes one vault edit. Every box picks the change up.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNo passwords on disk.\fR Passwords are fetched from Bitwarden, 1Password, \fBpass\fR, KeePassXC, an environment variable or any command. They are kept in memory for one run and never written or printed. Each box gets its own SSH key, so you can revoke one box without touching the others.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBClaude works on the network without seeing secrets.\fR \fBroster setup\fR writes \fBROSTER.md\fR, which tells Claude which hosts exist and how to reach them. Claude uses \fBssh\fR, \fBroster run\fR and \fBroster web\fR, and reads roster's data with \fB\-\-json\fR: roster supplies the credentials and Claude sees only the output.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBScans become inventory safely.\fR \fBroster discover\fR turns an nmap sweep into \fBroster.yml\fR entries marked \fBreviewed: false\fR\&. Nothing logs in to them until a person has reviewed them.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-v, \-\-version\fR
show program's version number and exit
.TP
\fB\-c, \-\-config PATH\fR
path to roster.yml (default: $ROSTER_CONFIG, ./roster.yml, the active named Inventory, then ~/.config/roster/roster.yml)
.TP
\fB\-\-inv NAME\fR
use the named Inventory NAME for this one command (`roster inv list` names them)
.SH COMMANDS
.SS init
.PP
\fBroster init [\-\-blank | \-\-inventory HOW] [\-\-name NAME]\fR
.PP
\-\-inventory HOW] [\-\-name NAME]`
.TP
\fB\-\-blank\fR
a short roster.yml with an empty group, instead of the commented example (same as \-\-inventory blank)
.TP
\fB\-\-inventory HOW\fR
blank, file:PATH (a copy of that roster.yml, once it loads), repo:URL (a git clone of the team's repo into roster.yml's folder, which must be empty) or ssh\-config[:PATH] (Hosts from ~/.ssh/config, or PATH: HostName, User, Port, IdentityFile)
.TP
\fB\-\-name NAME\fR
name the new Inventory too (`roster inv add NAME PATH`), e.g. beside one you keep
.SS setup
.PP
\fBroster setup [\-\-only LIST | \-\-host NAME] [\-\-dry\-run] [\-\-force] [\-\-key\-type TYPE] [\-\-rotate]\fR
.PP
One\-time per box: key, fingerprints, key install, SSH config, Claude files (\fB\-\-only\fR, \fB\-\-host NAME\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR, \fB\-\-key\-type TYPE\fR, \fB\-\-rotate\fR). \fB\-\-rotate\fR replaces the Box key on every Host, the new one proven on each before the old one's line goes (Rotating the Box key); plain \fBsetup\fR exits 1 while a rotation is pending. Each Host gets the key of its own \fBkey_type:\fR (one key per type on this Box, made on first need); a Host whose key type changed gets the new key, and loses the old one only once the new one logs in. \fB\-\-host NAME \-\-key\-type TYPE\fR tries TYPE on that Host for this run, over \fBroster.yml\fR (it needs \fB\-\-host\fR): its line goes in beside this Box's line of \fBroster.yml\fR\&'s type, which stays, so the next \fBroster sync\fR (which writes \fBroster.yml\fR\&'s type back) still logs in; another try takes the one before off; set \fBkey_type:\fR to keep it (FIPS Hosts). Where the key already works, a line of this Box's of another type that \fB~/.config/roster/key\-types\fR records there (a \fB\-\-key\-type\fR try's, a cleanup that failed) is taken off over the key login (T245). A type change puts the new key's line in, tests it, and only then takes the old type's line off; a Host that refuses the new type keeps the old line. \fB\-\-host NAME\fR is exactly the Host by that name, never a group, type or product NAME that \fB\-\-only NAME\fR would take too; like \fB\-\-only\fR it leaves the Mesh to \fBroster mesh\fR\&. After a rename, it re\-tags this Box's key where it works; a Host where another key's line already has the new name fails with 190 and is left as it is, unless \fB\-\-force\fR
.TP
\fB\-\-only LIST\fR
only these hosts, groups, types or products (comma\-separated)
.TP
\fB\-\-host NAME\fR
only the Host by exactly this name, never a group, type or product by it (\-\-only would take those too)
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
show what would happen, change nothing remote
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
on a renamed Box, go ahead on a Host where another Box's key line has the new name (its line is then this Box's to replace or revoke)
.TP
\fB\-\-key\-type TYPE\fR
with \-\-host: try this SSH key type there, over roster.yml's key_type: (ed25519, ecdsa, ecdsa\-256, ecdsa\-521, rsa, rsa\-BITS), for this run only; its line goes in beside the one of roster.yml's type, which stays. Set key_type: to keep it. With \-\-rotate: the type roster.yml gives the Hosts, checked
.TP
\fB\-\-rotate\fR
replace the Box key (this Box's own key, not a Mesh key: `roster mesh \-\-rotate HOST` does those) on every Host: a new key, proven on each Host before the old key's line goes, then the old key deleted. \-\-host NAME (a Host or a group) narrows it; a Host not reached stays pending until setup \-\-rotate runs again
.SS sync
.PP
\fBroster sync [\-\-no\-vault]\fR
.PP
Rewrite the SSH config and Claude files after editing \fBroster.yml\fR, and sync the vault too, as \fBvault sync\fR does (\fB\-\-no\-vault\fR: the map only). It touches no Host. It ends with one line: what changed in each (\fBvault: bw: 1 item changed (web\-01 ssh) since the last sync; map: 1 Host added (db\-01)\fR) and, only when something is left, \fBnext:\fR and the command that does it (\fBroster setup \-\-host db\-01 (no key on db\-01 yet)\fR, \fBroster add \-\-from\-vault\fR, \fBroster mesh\fR, \fBroster setup \-\-rotate\fR). A vault it couldn't sync doesn't fail it: locked with no terminal, it says so and names the provider's own unlock (\fBroster vault unlock\fR for Bitwarden); offline, it says when the vault last synced. In a terminal, with Bitwarden locked and no working saved session or \fBBW_SESSION\fR, it first says that unlocking there locks the roster app and any other bw session (as \fBroster vault unlock\fR does), then asks for the master password once and offers to keep it unlocked; Ctrl\-D skips the vault. Each run with a working session costs a \fBbw sync\fR (a cron job's \fBbin/roster\-pull\fR too); \fBupdate.sh\fR runs it with \fB\-\-no\-vault\fR\&. A Host taken out of \fBroster.yml\fR loses its ssh entry, with a note that this Box's key may still be on it; one renamed at the same address is said as renamed
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-vault\fR
the map only: leave the vault as last synced
.SS check
.PP
\fBroster check [\-\-box] [\-\-auth] [\-\-only LIST | \-\-host NAME] [\-\-json] [host]\fR
.PP
\-\-box \e
.TP
\fBHOST\fR
diagnose this one Host: inventory, ssh config, vault, DNS, port, host key, login (one connection)
.TP
\fB\-\-box\fR
check this Box instead, without connecting anywhere: roster.yml, ssh and its config, vault CLIs, askpass, the infra repo
.TP
\fB\-\-auth\fR
test each Login's password source (SSH, services, resources) instead, connecting to no Host and printing nothing secret
.TP
\fB\-\-only LIST\fR
only these hosts, groups, types or products (comma\-separated)
.TP
\fB\-\-host NAME\fR
check exactly this Host (never a group or type by the name); again for more, as setup's last line names them
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
print JSON for scripts and tools, never a secret (see Machine\-readable output in docs/cli\-reference.md)
.SS hosts
.PP
\fBroster hosts [\-\-resources] [\-\-only LIST] [\-\-find TEXT] [\-\-all] [\-\-long] [\-\-json] [NAME ...]\fR
.PP
The Hosts and resources in \fBroster.yml\fR that you can use: name, IP, user, logins (\fBssh (key), web (admin), api\fR, \fBwifi\fR) and title. Unreviewed scanned hosts are hidden unless you pass \fB\-\-all\fR, \fB\-\-find\fR or \fB\-\-only\fR\&. \fB\-\-long\fR shows the full table (type, product, web URL, scan description). \fBhosts NAME\fR (was \fBinfo\fR): everything about one Host or resource (for a key Host, \fBkey\fR: its key type and this Box's key file for it); fuzzy names work (\fBroster hosts core switch\fR), and it takes none of the list's flags. \fBhosts \-\-resources\fR (was \fBresources\fR): only the Wi\-Fi networks, accounts and secrets that aren't Hosts, with \fB\-\-only\fR taking resource names, types or groups and \fB\-\-find\fR searching every field
.TP
\fBNAME\fR
show everything about this one Host or resource; fuzzy words work
.TP
\fB\-\-resources\fR
list only the resources (Wi\-Fi networks, accounts and secrets with no address); \-\-only then takes resource names, types or groups, \-\-find every field
.TP
\fB\-\-only LIST\fR
only these hosts, groups, types or products (comma\-separated)
.TP
\fB\-\-find TEXT\fR
search name, title, description, type, product, group, ip
.TP
\fB\-\-all\fR
include unreviewed (scanned) hosts
.TP
\fB\-\-long\fR
full table: type, product, web URL, description
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
print JSON for scripts and tools, never a secret (see Machine\-readable output in docs/cli\-reference.md)
.SS run
.PP
\fBroster run [\-\-sudo] host ...\fR
.PP
Run a command; password\-only hosts get their password from the provider. \fB\-\-sudo\fR runs it as root, handing sudo the login's password (never shown). On an \fBos: windows\fR Host CMD always runs under PowerShell, whatever its DefaultShell, with its exit code passed on; \fB\-\-sudo\fR there is refused (159). See Windows Hosts
.TP
\fB\-\-sudo\fR
run it as root with sudo; roster gives sudo the login's password (never shown)
.TP
\fBHOST\fR
the host to run it on
.TP
\fBCOMMAND\fR
the command, after \-\-
.SS add
.PP
\fBroster add [\-\-ip ADDR] [\-\-user USER] [\-\-auth REF] [\-\-group GROUP] [\-\-enroll | \-\-no\-enroll] [\-\-os {windows,posix}] [\-\-title TEXT] [\-\-type TEXT] [\-\-port N] [\-\-from\-vault] [\-\-json] [\-\-all] [\-\-mark\-seen] [\-\-no\-sync] [\-\-setup] [\-\-dry\-run] [\-y] [NAME]\fR
.PP
The vault items no Login in \fBroster.yml\fR names, the new ones since the last run marked (synced first; \fB\-\-no\-sync\fR), and in a terminal a walk adding each [Y/n/edit/skip] the \fBroster add NAME\fR way; \fB\-\-all\fR every one, \fB\-\-json\fR the list, \fB\-\-mark\-seen\fR\&. Never reads a password. See Vault items not in roster.yml
.TP
\fBNAME\fR
the new Host's name: letters, digits, . _ \- (needed unless \-\-from\-vault)
.TP
\fB\-\-ip ADDR\fR
its address: an IPv4 or IPv6 address or a DNS name (asked for in a terminal when it's missing)
.TP
\fB\-\-user USER\fR
the SSH user (default: its group's or defaults')
.TP
\fB\-\-auth REF\fR
its Login: a vault item (bw:ITEM, op:...), env:VAR, prompt, key ... (default: its group's or defaults')
.TP
\fB\-\-group GROUP\fR
the group it goes in: an existing one, or a new one (default: the first group; asked in a terminal)
.TP
\fB\-\-enroll\fR
install this Box's key on it at setup (asked in a terminal; default: its group's)
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-enroll\fR
password logins only, no key
.TP
\fB\-\-os OS\fR
windows for a Windows Host with OpenSSH Server (default: posix)
.TP
\fB\-\-title TEXT\fR
a one\-line title
.TP
\fB\-\-type TEXT\fR
a free label (nas, switch, esxi, ...)
.TP
\fB\-\-port N\fR
its SSH port (default: 22)
.TP
\fB\-\-from\-vault\fR
fill \-\-ip (and \-\-port) from the \-\-auth item's URI and \-\-user from its username, never reading its password (bw, op); flags given win. With no NAME: list the vault items no Login in roster.yml names (new ones since the last run), and in a terminal add each one [Y/n/edit/skip]
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
with \-\-from\-vault and no NAME: print the list as JSON, add nothing
.TP
\fB\-\-all\fR
with \-\-from\-vault and no NAME: every item roster.yml doesn't name, not only the new ones
.TP
\fB\-\-mark\-seen\fR
with \-\-from\-vault and no NAME: remember every item listed as seen, so it isn't new next time
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-sync\fR
with \-\-from\-vault and no NAME: don't sync the vault first (bw sync)
.TP
\fB\-\-setup\fR
then run setup for it alone (its host key, and this Box's key on it when enrolled) and check it, and say how each went
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
print the lines it would add; write nothing
.TP
\fB\-y, \-\-yes\fR
never ask: a missing value it can't default is an error naming its flag
.SS discover
.PP
\fBroster discover [\-\-write] [\-\-group NAME] [\-\-ports PORTS] [\-\-top\-ports N] [\-\-no\-ping] [\-\-os] [\-\-builtin] [\-\-timeout SECONDS] [\-\-force] [\-\-json] CIDR [CIDR ...]\fR
.PP
Scan networks; \fB\-\-write\fR adds the results to \fBroster.yml\fR, then syncs this Box as \fBroster sync\fR does
.TP
\fBCIDR\fR
e.g. 10.0.1.0/24 (several allowed)
.TP
\fB\-\-write\fR
add the new Hosts it finds to roster.yml, unreviewed, for you to name and review (default: preview only)
.TP
\fB\-\-group NAME\fR
group name for new hosts (default: discovered\-YYYYMMDD)
.TP
\fB\-\-ports PORTS\fR
TCP ports to scan (default: common server, web, database, remote\-admin and network\-gear ports)
.TP
\fB\-\-top\-ports N\fR
nmap only: scan nmap's N most common ports instead
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-ping\fR
nmap only: scan hosts that don't answer ping (slower)
.TP
\fB\-\-os\fR
nmap only: OS detection (needs sudo)
.TP
\fB\-\-builtin\fR
use the built\-in scanner even if nmap is installed
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout SECONDS\fR
built\-in scanner connect timeout (default: 0.5)
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
allow scanning more than 4096 addresses
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
print JSON for scripts and tools, never a secret (see Machine\-readable output in docs/cli\-reference.md)
.SS web
.PP
\fBroster web [\-d BODY] [\-H HEADER] [\-k] [\-\-timeout SECONDS] HOST [SERVICE] METHOD PATH [HOST [SERVICE] METHOD PATH ...]\fR
.PP
HTTP request to a host's web service, logged in with its stored credentials (\fB\-d BODY\fR, \fB\-H 'Name: value'\fR, \fB\-k\fR)
.TP
\fBHOST [SERVICE] METHOD PATH\fR
e.g. esxi GET /folder  or  orion3 https GET /api/status
.TP
\fB\-d, \-\-data BODY\fR
request body (@FILE reads a file)
.TP
\fB\-H, \-\-header HEADER\fR
extra header 'Name: value' (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\-k, \-\-insecure\fR
don't verify the TLS certificate
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout SECONDS\fR
how long to wait for the answer (default: 30)
.SS mesh
.PP
\fBroster mesh [\-\-dry\-run] [\-\-rotate HOST] [\-y] [\-\-all] [\-\-remove] [\-\-json] [{cut,status,leftovers}] [HOST]\fR
.PP
Set up Host\-to\-Host access as \fBmesh:\fR and \fBlinks:\fR say, one Host at a time: a key made on each source, tagged entries on its peers. A Link's \fBvia: HOST\fR goes through a jump Host (\fBProxyJump\fR), tested through it. \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR shows the plan, \fB\-\-rotate\fR replaces a Host's Mesh key. \fBmesh status\fR shows who reaches whom without connecting; \fBmesh cut HOST [\-y]\fR takes a Host out everywhere at once and writes \fBmesh: false\fR for it as a Pending change, then syncs this Box as \fBroster sync\fR does; \fBmesh cut \-\-all [\-y]\fR takes this Box's Mesh entries (and an older roster's untagged ones) off every Host and leaves \fBroster.yml\fR as it is (before removing roster: Uninstalling roster); \fBmesh leftovers\fR (was \fB\-\-find\-leftovers\fR) lists the Mesh entries on every Host that this \fBroster.yml\fR doesn't ask for (and files by a Mesh key's name in the Mesh's folders that aren't a key roster made, which it never removes), changing nothing, and \fBmesh leftovers \-\-remove [\-y]\fR (was \fB\-\-remove\-leftovers\fR) removes them after a question (an older roster's may be another Box's: Whose Mesh it is); \fB\-\-json\fR with \fBstatus\fR or \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR\&. See The Mesh
.TP
\fBACTION\fR
cut HOST: take it out of the Mesh now, everywhere; status: who reaches whom (no connections); leftovers: look on every Host, once, for Mesh entries this roster.yml doesn't ask for (this Box's, or an older roster's with no Box in the tag); changes nothing without \-\-remove
.TP
\fBHOST\fR
with cut: the Host to cut out
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
show the plan; change nothing on any Host
.TP
\fB\-\-rotate HOST\fR
replace HOST's Mesh key (repeatable)
.TP
\fB\-y, \-\-yes\fR
with cut or leftovers \-\-remove: don't ask first
.TP
\fB\-\-all\fR
with cut: every Mesh entry off every Host, roster.yml unchanged (before removing roster from this Box)
.TP
\fB\-\-remove\fR
with leftovers: then remove them after a question (\-y doesn't ask): an older roster's may be another Box's Mesh
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
with status or \-\-dry\-run: print JSON for scripts and tools, never a secret (see Machine\-readable output in docs/cli\-reference.md)
.SS revoke
.PP
\fBroster revoke [\-\-only LIST] [\-\-all] [\-y]\fR
.PP
Remove this box's key from every host (its line under any name it had, found by its public key: the key is one per Box, so revoking one Inventory also takes it off a machine another Inventory lists, under any \fBbox_name:\fR, and says so: \fBroster setup\fR for that Inventory puts it back, or use \fB\-\-all\fR); it names this box's files to delete (with \fB~/.config/roster/key\-name*\fR, the name setup recorded for its key's lines) only once every Host of this \fBroster.yml\fR has lost the key (never with \fB\-\-only\fR, never another \fBroster.yml\fR\&'s \fBconfig.d/roster\fR, and never a key file another Inventory on this Box uses too: that one is kept, with a note naming the Inventory, 221), and exits 1 when a Host was skipped or none was reached. \fB\-\-all\fR: from every Inventory's Hosts on this box (each named one and the unnamed one) at once, with one question and one summary; a Host \fBssh NAME\fR doesn't reach (left out, or its Inventory not synced here) is skipped and said. See Named Inventories. On an \fBos: windows\fR Host it cleans both files an administrator's key could be in. A Host it couldn't revoke is listed with why
.TP
\fB\-\-only LIST\fR
only these hosts, groups, types or products (comma\-separated)
.TP
\fB\-\-all\fR
every Inventory on this box: each named one and the unnamed one, with one question and one summary
.TP
\fB\-y, \-\-yes\fR
don't ask first
.SS inv
.PP
\fBroster inv [\-\-json] {list,add,use,remove,which} [NAME] [PATH]\fR
.PP
add NAME PATH\e
.TP
\fBACTION\fR
list (the active one marked *), add NAME PATH, use NAME (make it the active one), remove NAME (forget the name; the file stays), which (print the roster.yml roster uses)
.TP
\fBNAME\fR
the Inventory's name: letters, digits, . _ \-
.TP
\fBPATH\fR
with add: its roster.yml
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
print JSON for scripts and tools, never a secret (see Machine\-readable output in docs/cli\-reference.md)
.SS vault
.PP
\fBroster vault ACTION ...\fR
.PP
roster's own views of the vault, one action each (below); plain \fBroster vault\fR lists them. One home per provider: \fBvault bw\fR is Bitwarden's own CLI, \fBvault op\fR 1Password's
.SS vault status
.PP
\fBroster vault status [\-\-json]\fR
.PP
(Was \fBproviders\fR\&.) The password providers here (built\-in, plugins, \fBroster.yml\fR\&'s), whether their tools are installed and which groups use them, unknown ones in use (exit 1), then each vault's state (installed, logged in, locked or unlocked: the same checks as \fBroster check \-\-box\fR\&'s Vault section, one source) and when \fBroster vault sync\fR last synced each one that syncs ("last synced 3 h ago", or "never synced by roster")
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
print JSON for scripts and tools, never a secret (see Machine\-readable output in docs/cli\-reference.md)
.SS vault sync
.PP
\fBroster vault sync\fR
.PP
Sync every vault \fBroster.yml\fR\&'s Logins use that keeps a local copy (Bitwarden: \fBbw sync\fR; 1Password and the others ask their server or file each time, so it says they need none), then say what changed since the last sync: the items added and changed by name, the removed ones by count. Never a password: see The vault isn't watched. Nothing changed: \fBup to date\fR\&. An item added that no Login in \fBroster.yml\fR names ends it with \fBnext: roster add \-\-from\-vault\fR\&. Exits 1 if a sync failed, saying when roster last synced it
.SS vault unlock
.PP
\fBroster vault unlock [PROVIDER]\fR
.PP
Unlock Bitwarden and keep it unlocked for every roster run on this Box: asks the master password (it goes to \fBbw\fR in an environment variable, never on a command line), then saves the session key in \fB~/.config/roster/bw\-session\fR (a new file renamed into place, mode 0600; a file there that's a symlink or isn't yours is refused, 202). It lasts until \fBroster vault lock\fR, \fBbw lock\fR, or the roster app's lock. Already unlocked by a working saved session: says so, asks nothing; a working \fBBW_SESSION\fR in your shell is saved as it is, with no new unlock (which would lock it and the app). Bitwarden only for now (203 for \fBkp\fR, which keeps no session, and \fBop\fR). It ends with the \fBnext:\fR line \fBroster sync\fR would give, when something is left (a \fBroster setup\fR the locked vault held up). See Staying unlocked
.TP
\fBPROVIDER\fR
bw (the default and, for now, the only one: KeePassXC keeps no session, and 1Password's op signin keeps its own)
.SS vault lock
.PP
\fBroster vault lock [PROVIDER]\fR
.PP
\fBbw lock\fR, then remove \fB~/.config/roster/bw\-session\fR (even if bw failed or isn't installed; each is reported on its own). bw keeps one unlock at a time, so every bw session on this Box is locked, the roster app's too. Bitwarden only for now (203)
.TP
\fBPROVIDER\fR
bw (the default and, for now, the only one)
.SS vault reveal
.PP
\fBroster vault reveal [\-\-wifi] name\fR
.PP
(Was \fBreveal\fR\&.) Print a resource's secret for a person to type in, not for Claude (\fB\-\-wifi\fR: a QR\-ready \fBWIFI:\fR string)
.TP
\fBNAME\fR
the resource
.TP
\fB\-\-wifi\fR
print a WIFI:... string for a QR code instead
.SS vault bw
.PP
\fBroster vault bw [\-\-write] ...\fR
.PP
(Was \fBbw\fR\&.) The Bitwarden CLI itself with this box's vault session from \fB~/.config/roster/bw\-session\fR (never prompts, except \fBlogin\fR and \fBunlock\fR), e.g. \fBroster vault bw \-\- list items \-\-search web\-01\fR\&. Read\-only unless \fB\-\-write\fR: commands that change the vault, write a file on this Box or act on the account need it, and commands roster doesn't know are refused. roster never reads what bw prints. See \fBroster vault bw\fR
.TP
\fB\-\-write\fR
allow bw commands that change the vault, write a file here or act on the account (create, edit, delete, export, receive, \-\-output, login, logout, unlock, lock, config, ...)
.TP
\fBBW_ARGS\fR
arguments for bw, after \-\-
.SS vault op
.PP
\fBroster vault op [\-\-write] ...\fR
.PP
The 1Password CLI itself with your op sign\-in (the 1Password app's CLI integration, or an \fBOP_SESSION_*\fR or \fBOP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN\fR in your shell), e.g. \fBroster vault op \-\- item list \-\-vault Infra\fR\&. Read\-only unless \fB\-\-write\fR: commands that change the vault or the account, or write a file on this Box, need it; \fBop run\fR, \fB\-\-session\fR and \fB\-\-config\fR are refused; commands roster doesn't know are refused. roster never reads what op prints. See \fBroster vault op\fR
.TP
\fB\-\-write\fR
allow op commands that change the vault or the account or write a file here (item create, item edit, item delete, item share, \-\-out\-file, signin, signout, account add, update, ...)
.TP
\fBOP_ARGS\fR
arguments for op, after \-\-
.SS help
.PP
\fBroster help [\-\-man] [\-\-install] [\-\-prefix DIR] [\-\-completion SHELL] [COMMAND ...]\fR
.PP
\fBroster help\fR: the overview. \fBroster help COMMAND\fR (\fBhelp vault bw\fR): that command's options and examples, as \fB\-\-help\fR\&. \fB\-\-man\fR (was \fBman\fR): show the \fBroster(1)\fR man page; with \fB\-\-install\fR it copies it to \fBPREFIX/share/man/man1/roster.1\fR, next to \fBPREFIX/bin/roster\fR (\fB~/.local\fR after \fBuv tool install\fR), where \fBman roster\fR finds it; into a prefix you can't write to, it says so and exits 1 (pick \fB\-\-prefix ~/.local\fR, or use sudo). \fBinstall.sh\fR does this for you. `\-\-completion bash\e
.TP
\fBCOMMAND\fR
the command (and its action: vault bw) to show the help of
.TP
\fB\-\-man\fR
show the roster(1) man page
.TP
\fB\-\-install\fR
with \-\-man: copy it to PREFIX/share/man/man1/, next to PREFIX/bin/roster (~/.local for uv tool install), where `man roster` finds it
.TP
\fB\-\-prefix DIR\fR
with \-\-man \-\-install: install into DIR/share/man/man1/ instead
.TP
\fB\-\-completion SHELL\fR
print Tab completion for bash, zsh, fish or powershell (install.sh turns it on)
.SS serve
.PP
\fBroster serve\fR
.PP
Internal, run by the app: the JSON\-RPC API the desktop app talks to, on stdin/stdout (see \fBroster serve\fR API)
.SS askpass
.PP
\fBroster askpass [prompt]\fR
.PP
Internal, run by ssh: the \fBSSH_ASKPASS\fR helper for a \fBserve\fR password Session
.TP
\fBPROMPT\fR
the prompt ssh shows
.SH JSON OUTPUT
.PP
The read commands take \fB\-\-json\fR, for scripts, other tools and Claude: \fBhosts\fR (with \fB\-\-only\fR, \fB\-\-find\fR, \fB\-\-all\fR), \fBadd \-\-from\-vault\fR (no NAME: the list), \fBhosts NAME\fR, \fBhosts \-\-resources\fR, \fBcheck\fR, \fBcheck HOST\fR, \fBcheck \-\-box\fR, \fBcheck \-\-auth\fR, \fBdiscover\fR (its preview, without \fB\-\-write\fR), \fBmesh status\fR, \fBmesh \-\-dry\-run\fR (the plan) and \fBvault status\fR\&. The CLI is roster's interface for other apps; \fBroster serve\fR is the desktop app's own backend, not a public API. With \fB\-\-json\fR, stdout is one JSON document on one line, with no colour, table or warning, and the exit status is the same as without it.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNever a secret.\fR Logins are references: the provider and the item (\fBbw:esx\-02 ssh\fR, \fBop:Lab/esx\-02\fR, \fBprompt\fR, \fBagent\fR). A \fBcmd:\fR or \fBenv:\fR reference says how a secret is fetched, and a password pasted into a \fBcmd:\fR line would show, so each one is cut to its kind (\fBcmd:\fR, \fBenv:\fR) everywhere in the output, error messages and check messages included, the way Plugins get them: a \fBcmd:\fR one to the end of its line, an \fBenv:\fR one its variable's name, wherever one starts a text or follows a space, a quote or one of \fB=:,({[\fR\&. An error message keeps a trailing \fB(code N)\fR\&. The cases both implementations are tested against are in \fBtests/vectors/redaction.json\fR\&. \fBcheck \-\-auth \-\-json\fR fetches every secret it tests and says only whether it was found. \fBvault reveal\fR has no \fB\-\-json\fR: it's for a person.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBOne shape.\fR A Host, a group and a resource are the objects \fBroster serve\fR answers with (\fBhosts.list\fR, \fBhost.get\fR; the serve API lists their fields): every field \fBroster.yml\fR resolves to, with \fBssh: {user, port, auth}\fR, \fBlogin: {user, auth}\fR, \fBservices\fR by name, and \fBproblems\fR, the service entries left out (the warnings the table prints on stderr).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBA version.\fR \fBversion\fR is the first field of every document, errors included. It is \fB1\fR now.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBErrors\fR go to stderr as \fB{"version": 1, "error": {"code", "message", "fix"}}\fR, with nothing on stdout. The codes are \fBroster serve\fR\&'s: \fB1\fR \fBroster.yml\fR wasn't found or doesn't load (\fBfix\fR says where), \fB2\fR no Host or resource by that name, \fB3\fR any other refusal. Wrong arguments are code \fB3\fR too, with exit status 2. A refusal serve has its own code for keeps it: \fB121\fR too many addresses for \fBdiscover\fR, \fB110\fR a \fB\-\-rotate\fR Host that isn't a Mesh source.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBWhat's a breaking change:\fR removing or renaming a field, changing its type or what it means, or changing an exit status. Those come with a new \fBversion\fR and a line under \fBBreaking\fR in What's new. A new field or a new command with \fB\-\-json\fR isn't one, so read the fields you need and ignore the rest.
.TP
\fBadd \-\-from\-vault \-\-json\fR
\fB{version, items: [{provider, id, name, auth, new, host?, address?, port?, user?, note?}], unmapped, new}\fR: the new items (every one with \fB\-\-all\fR) no Login names, the Host each suggests and the Login to write; \fBunmapped\fR and \fBnew\fR count them all. Never a value. Errors: \fB14\fR locked, \fB187\fR the listing failed, \fB188\fR no vault to list
.TP
\fBhosts \-\-json\fR
\fB{version, hosts: [Host], resources: [resource], groups: {name: {title, description}}, hidden}\fR\&. Hosts and resources in \fBroster.yml\fR order, filtered as the table is; \fBhidden\fR counts the unreviewed Hosts left out (\fB\-\-all\fR shows them). Exit 1 when \fB\-\-find\fR matches nothing
.TP
\fBhosts NAME \-\-json\fR
\fB{version, host: Host, group}\fR or \fB{version, resource: resource, group}\fR; \fBgroup\fR is \fB{name, title, description}\fR, or \fBnull\fR for a resource with none
.TP
\fBhosts \-\-resources \-\-json\fR
\fB{version, resources: [resource]}\fR\&. Exit 1 when \fB\-\-only\fR or \fB\-\-find\fR matches nothing
.TP
\fBcheck \-\-json\fR
\fB{version, hosts: [{name, ok, ssh, checks}], mesh, ok}\fR: \fBchecks\fR is \fB{ssh: bool, SERVICE: bool, ...}\fR, \fBssh\fR says what was tested (\fBlogin\fR with this Box's key, \fBport\fR for a password\-only Host). \fBmesh\fR is the drift (the plan \fBroster mesh \-\-dry\-run\fR would show), or \fBnull\fR with no Mesh or with \fB\-\-only\fR\&. \fBrotation_pending\fR, \fBrotation_problems\fR (T240) and \fBkey_type_drift\fR (\fB{host: why}\fR, 219: a Host holding this Box's line of another type than \fBroster.yml\fR\&'s, T245) fail it too. Exit 1 unless \fBok\fR
.TP
\fBcheck HOST \-\-json\fR
\fB{version, host, ok, steps: [{step, status, message, code?, fix?, details?, data?}]}\fR, as \fBhost.check\fR: \fBstatus\fR is \fBok\fR, \fBfail\fR or \fBskipped\fR\&. An unknown Host is a failed \fBinventory\fR step (code 2), not an error. Exit 1 unless \fBok\fR
.TP
\fBcheck \-\-box \-\-json\fR
\fB{version, checks: [{section, level, message, fix?, topic?, saved_session?}], ok}\fR, as \fBbox.check\fR: \fBlevel\fR is \fBok\fR, \fBwarn\fR or \fBerror\fR; \fBsaved_session\fR on a Bitwarden Vault check is \fBnone\fR, \fBworking\fR, \fBexpired\fR, \fBignored\fR or \fBunknown\fR (Staying unlocked; never in \fBbox.check\fR). Exit 1 if one is an \fBerror\fR
.TP
\fBdiscover CIDR... \-\-json\fR
\fB{version, targets, addresses, ports, scanner, count, quiet, found: [Found], group}\fR: the preview, after the scan. \fBtargets\fR, \fBaddresses\fR (how many it visits), \fBports\fR (how many) and \fBscanner\fR (\fBnmap\fR or \fBbuilt\-in\fR) as \fBdiscover.plan\fR; \fBcount\fR, \fBquiet\fR and \fBfound\fR as \fBdiscover.status\fR (a Found is \fB{address, name, type, hostname, title, web, known, added, vendor, os, mac, ports}\fR: \fBname\fR the name \fB\-\-write\fR would give it, \fBnull\fR when \fBknown\fR names the Host already at that address; \fBadded\fR is always \fBnull\fR here). \fBgroup\fR is the group \fB\-\-write\fR would add the new ones to. Not with \fB\-\-write\fR (exit 2). Over 4,096 addresses without \fB\-\-force\fR: error \fB121\fR\&. A \fBroster.yml\fR that's there but doesn't load (named by \fB\-c\fR, \fBROSTER_CONFIG\fR or found in the usual places): error \fB1\fR with its fix; with none at all (or \fBROSTER_CONFIG\fR naming no file) it scans anyway
.TP
\fBinv list \-\-json\fR
\fB{version, active, inventories: [{name, path, active, exists}]}\fR, as \fBinventories.list\fR
.TP
\fBinv which \-\-json\fR
\fB{version, path, name, exists}\fR: the \fBroster.yml\fR roster uses and its name among the named Inventories (\fBnull\fR if it isn't one). Exit 1 if it isn't there
.TP
\fBmesh status \-\-json\fR
\fB{version, links, excluded, reach, members, pending, cut_pending, out_of_date, warnings}\fR, as \fBmesh.status\fR
.TP
\fBmesh \-\-dry\-run \-\-json\fR
\fB{version, applied: false, links, hosts: [{host, changes, status, error?}], excluded, reach, pending, already, warnings}\fR, as \fBmesh.plan\fR: it connects to the Hosts, one at a time, and changes nothing. Exit 1 if a Host is \fBpending\fR (didn't answer). \fB\-\-json\fR goes with \fBstatus\fR and \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR only, never with an apply or \fBcut\fR
.TP
\fBvault status \-\-json\fR
\fB{version, providers: [{name, kind, help, tool, installed, used_by}], unknown, ok, vault: [{section, level, message, fix?, topic?, saved_session?}], last_sync: {provider: time}}\fR: \fBvault\fR is the Vault section of \fBcheck \-\-box \-\-json\fR, \fBlast_sync\fR when \fBroster vault sync\fR last synced each provider that syncs (\fBnull\fR: never); \fBtool\fR is the CLI it runs (\fBnull\fR for none, and \fBinstalled\fR \fBnull\fR with it), \fBused_by\fR the groups whose Logins use it, \fBunknown\fR the providers \fBroster.yml\fR names that aren't set up. Exit 1 if there's one
.TP
\fBcheck \-\-auth \-\-json\fR
\fB{version, logins: [{group, auth, users, used_for: [{service, hosts}], status, error?}], ok}\fR: one row per Login reference and group; \fBstatus\fR is \fBok\fR (a value was found; it isn't tested against a Host), \fBfail\fR (with \fBerror\fR) or \fBask\fR (\fBprompt\fR). Exit 1 if one failed
.PP
Wrapped and cut short here (\fB\&...\fR); roster prints each document on one line:
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
$ roster hosts \-\-only esx\-02 \-\-json
{"version": 1, "hosts": [{"name": "esx\-02", "group": "lab", "ip": "10.0.0.5", "user": "root", "port": 22,
  "enroll": false, "reviewed": true, "title": "Lab ESXi", "ssh": {"port": 22, "user": "root",
  "auth": "bw:esx\-02 ssh"}, "services": {}, "problems": [], ...}], "resources": [],
  "groups": {"lab": {"title": "The lab", "description": ""}}, "hidden": 0}
$ roster check esx\-02 \-\-json
{"version": 1, "host": "esx\-02", "ok": false, "steps": [{"step": "inventory", "status": "ok", "message": "root@10.0.0.5:22 (password login)"},
  ..., {"step": "tcp", "status": "fail", "code": 42, "message": "...", "fix": "..."}, ...]}
$ roster hosts nope \-\-json
{"version": 1, "error": {"code": 2, "message": "unknown host or resource 'nope'", "fix": null}}   (on stderr, exit 1)
.RE
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
\fB\&./roster.yml\fR
The Inventory: hosts, groups, resources and vault references, never passwords. roster looks for it in this order: \fB\-c PATH\fR, \fB\-\-inv NAME\fR, \fB$ROSTER_CONFIG\fR, \fB\&./roster.yml\fR, the active named Inventory, \fB~/.config/roster/roster.yml\fR (\fB%APPDATA%\eroster\eroster.yml\fR on Windows)
.TP
\fB~/.config/roster/\fR
This box's roster folder (on Windows, \fB%APPDATA%\eroster\fR, next to the app's \fBinit.lua\fR): \fBroster.yml\fR if you keep it here, \fBproviders/\fR for provider plugins, \fBbw\-session\fR
.TP
\fB~/.config/roster/bw\-session\fR
A saved Bitwarden session (\fBbw unlock \-\-raw\fR), used only if it's yours alone: not a symlink, owned by you, and only you can read it (on Windows: owned by you, and nobody but you, SYSTEM and Administrators may open it)
.TP
\fB~/.ssh/roster_ed25519\fR
This box's SSH key (\fBkey_path:\fR in \fBroster.yml\fR moves it), made by \fBroster setup\fR
.TP
\fB~/.ssh/roster_ecdsa\fR, \fBroster_rsa\fR, \fBroster_<type>\fR
This box's key of another key type, for the Hosts whose \fBkey_type:\fR asks for it, beside \fBkey_path:\fR (one per type, the same \fBroster\-managed:<box>\fR tag), made by \fBroster setup\fR on first need (FIPS Hosts)
.TP
\fB~/.ssh/roster_ed25519.new\fR, \fB\&.old\fR
During a Box key rotation: the new key before it takes \fBkey_path:\fR\&'s place, and the old one, kept only while a Host is pending, then deleted (Rotating the Box key)
.TP
\fB~/.config/roster/key\-rotation\fR
The Hosts a Box key rotation is pending on (a named Inventory's: \fBkey\-rotation\-NAME\fR); gone once it's done
.TP
\fB~/.config/roster/key\-types\fR
The key types of this Box's lines on each Host, as \fBroster setup\fR (and a rotation) last left them (a named Inventory's: \fBkey\-types\-NAME\fR; T245): \fBcheck\fR and \fBsync\fR warn (219) when a Host holds another type than \fBroster.yml\fR\&'s, and \fBrevoke\fR takes its Hosts off
.TP
\fB~/.config/roster/setup\-left\fR
The Hosts the last \fBroster setup\fR that reached them didn't finish, by name (a named Inventory's: \fBsetup\-left\-NAME\fR), so \fBsync\fR and \fBvault unlock\fR name \fBroster setup \-\-host\fR for them; gone once none is left
.TP
\fB~/.config/roster/key\-created\fR
When roster made or rotated each of this Box's keys, for \fBkey_max_age:\fR
.TP
\fB~/.config/roster/run.lock\fR
The lock one run that changes Hosts at a time holds (a named Inventory's: \fBrun\-NAME.lock\fR): its pid and command, nothing secret
.TP
\fB~/.ssh/roster_known_hosts\fR
Host keys recorded on first contact (a named Inventory's: \fB~/.ssh/roster_known_hosts\-NAME\fR)
.TP
\fB~/.ssh/config.d/roster\fR
The SSH config entries \fBroster setup\fR and \fBroster sync\fR write (a named Inventory's: \fB~/.ssh/roster\-inventories/NAME\fR)
.TP
\fB~/.ssh/config.d/roster.inventories\fR
With named Inventories: what \fB~/.ssh/config\fR includes, every Inventory's Hosts with the active one's first (a name an earlier one has is left out)
.TP
\fB~/.config/roster/vault\-sync/PROVIDER.json\fR
What \fBroster vault sync\fR compares against (0600): each login item's id and revision date and when it was taken, never a name or value (The vault isn't watched)
.TP
\fB~/.config/roster/inventories.json\fR
The named Inventories and which one is active (\fBroster inv\fR)
.TP
\fBROSTER.md\fR, \fBCLAUDE.md\fR, \fB\&.claude/settings.json\fR
What Claude reads, written next to \fBroster.yml\fR by \fBsetup\fR and \fBsync\fR
.TP
\fB~/.config/roster/mesh.json\fR
What \fBroster mesh\fR couldn't finish (pending Hosts, cuts pending) and when it last applied, on this box (a named Inventory's: \fB~/.config/roster/mesh\-NAME.json\fR)
.TP
\fB~/.ssh/roster\-mesh/\fR (or the Host's \fBmesh_dir:\fR; on ESXi, \fB\&.roster\-mesh\fR on a datastore), \fB~/ROSTER.md\fR (on Hosts)
A Mesh source's key, known hosts, ssh config and its peers for Claude, written by \fBroster mesh\fR (The Mesh)
.TP
\fB$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/roster/\fR
Short\-lived private state: kept web logins and the askpass socket (on Windows, the askpass channel is a named pipe and its folder is in \fB%TEMP%\fR). Without \fB$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR\fR, kept web logins go in \fB~/.cache/roster/sessions/\fR, which survives a logout: each one is deleted when it's found expired (10 minutes at most) or rejected
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\fBROSTER_CONFIG\fR
The \fBroster.yml\fR to use when neither \fB\-c\fR nor \fB\-\-inv\fR is given (it comes before the active named Inventory)
.TP
\fBBW_SESSION\fR, \fBBW_PASSWORD\fR
An unlocked Bitwarden session, or the master password to unlock with
.TP
\fBBW_CLIENTID\fR, \fBBW_CLIENTSECRET\fR
A Bitwarden API key, for headless boxes
.TP
\fBOP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN\fR
A 1Password service account, for headless boxes
.TP
\fBXDG_RUNTIME_DIR\fR
Where roster keeps its short\-lived private state
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
\fB0\fR
Success
.TP
\fB1\fR
An error (roster prints it on stderr, starting \fBerror:\fR), a check that failed, or a web request answered with HTTP 400 or above
.TP
\fB2\fR
Wrong arguments
.TP
\fB130\fR
Interrupted with Ctrl+C
.PP
With \fB\-\-json\fR, the status is the same, and an error is JSON on stderr (Machine\-readable output). \fBroster run\fR exits with the remote command's status, \fBroster vault bw\fR with \fBbw\fR\&'s, and \fBroster vault op\fR with \fBop\fR\&'s. \fBroster mesh\fR and \fBroster mesh cut\fR (\fB\-\-all\fR too) exit 1 while a Host is pending, and \fBmesh leftovers [\-\-remove]\fR when a Host didn't answer. \fBroster check\fR and \fBroster revoke\fR exit 1 when a Host was skipped (\fBssh NAME\fR doesn't reach it from this Box) or none was checked or revoked. A \fBroster discover \-\-write\fR or \fBroster mesh cut\fR whose sync of this Box failed afterwards keeps its edit and its exit status, and says so on stderr: `\fBwarning: this Box's ssh config didn't follow roster.yml (why): run \fRroster sync\fB on this box\fR`.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
roster init                              # write a commented roster.yml to start from
roster init \-\-blank                      # ... or a short, empty one (\-\-inventory ssh\-config: your ~/.ssh/config's Hosts)
roster setup \-\-dry\-run                   # which hosts would get this box's key
roster hosts \-\-find esx                  # search names, titles, types, addresses
roster hosts esx\-02                      # everything about one host
roster check esx\-02                      # why a Session to esx\-02 would or wouldn't open
roster check \-\-box                       # this Box's health: roster.yml, ssh, vault, infra repo
roster run esx\-02 \-\- uptime              # run a command with the stored login
roster web esx\-02 GET /api/status        # an HTTP request, logged in
roster add nas\-01 \-\-ip 10.0.5.50 \-\-auth bw:nas\-01 \-\-setup   # one Host you know, then set it up
roster discover 10.0.5.0/24              # scan a network, preview only
roster vault reveal lab\-guest \-\-wifi | qrencode \-t ansiutf8
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
\fBssh\fR(1), \fBssh_config\fR(5), \fBnmap\fR(1), \fBbw\fR(1)
.PP
The full reference, with the roster.yml format and the security model: docs/cli\-reference.md in roster's source, https://github.com/2styx2/roster
